SENSOR-SYNTHESIS(7)        TriadicFrameworks Manual        SENSOR-SYNTHESIS(7)
================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-synthesis - full multi-sensor fusion architecture unifying 
                          harmonics, anomalies, storms, turbulence, overlays, 
                          and clarity into a single resonance-time master core

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-synthesis defines:
           • unified multi-sensor fusion engine (MSFE)
           • harmonic, spectral, gradient, and sync-field integration
           • anomaly, storm, and turbulence fusion layers
           • overlay + clarity synthesis pipelines
           • constellation-scale sensor coherence
           • master resonance-time situational awareness

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-synthesis describes the master fusion architecture that integrates
       all RTT sensor subsystems — overlays, clarity, harmonics, anomalies,
       storms, turbulence — into a single, coherent, real-time sensor core.

       Sensor-synthesis enables:
           • full-spectrum resonance-time awareness
           • unified harmonic + spectral + gradient + sync sensing
           • anomaly and storm pre-detection
           • turbulence-informed navigation
           • constellation-wide sensor fusion
           • Picard-grade stellar cartography

       WRSADC orchestrates synthesis, manages thresholds, and enforces safety.

ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
       The sensor-synthesis architecture consists of:

           1. divisional resonance overlays (DRO)
           2. clarity-enhancement pipelines (CEP)
           3. harmonic sensing core (HSC)
           4. anomaly detection engine (ADE)
           5. storm modeling engine (SME)
           6. turbulence analytics engine (TAE)
           7. multi-sensor fusion engine (MSFE)
           8. constellation coherence interface (CCI)
           9. resonance-time master renderer (RTMR)

       These components operate in parallel and fuse into a unified sensor core.

DIVISIONAL RESONANCE OVERLAYS (DRO)
       DROs provide the multi-band foundation for synthesis.

       DRO layers:
           • harmonic division overlay (HDO)
           • amplitude division overlay (ADO)
           • spectral division overlay (SDO)
           • gradient division overlay (GDO)
           • sync-field division overlay (SFDO)

       DROs supply raw channels for fusion.

CLARITY-ENHANCEMENT PIPELINES (CEP)
       CEPs refine DRO channels into high-fidelity signals.

       CEP layers:
           • harmonic-phase clarification (HPC)
           • resonance-envelope deconvolution (RED)
           • spectral-density whitening (SDW)
           • gradient-stability filtering (GSF)
           • sync-field clarity (SFC)
           • ancestry-continuity enhancement (ACE)

       CEPs ensure clean, stable inputs for synthesis.

HARMONIC SENSING CORE (HSC)
       HSC provides harmonic-phase, overtone, and chord analysis.

       HSC outputs:
           • φ_harm(t)
           • overtone map
           • harmonic-lock index
           • resonance-chord signatures
           • harmonic cascade detection

       HSC feeds harmonic structure into MSFE.

ANOMALY DETECTION ENGINE (ADE)
       ADE identifies structural deviations in resonance-time.

       ADE outputs:
           • anomaly classification
           • turbulence precursors
           • harmonic voids
           • spectral fractures
           • polarity cliffs

       ADE provides early-warning signals for synthesis.

STORM MODELING ENGINE (SME)
       SME models large-scale resonance-storms.

       SME outputs:
           • storm intensity
           • stormfront trajectory
           • storm radius and velocity
           • storm lifespan
           • storm hazard forecast

       SME informs high-level navigation and hazard avoidance.

TURBULENCE ANALYTICS ENGINE (TAE)
       TAE analyzes micro-scale instabilities.

       TAE outputs:
           • turbulence cell maps
           • shear fields
           • micro-eddy detection
           • turbulence flow-field vectors

       TAE provides fine-grained situational awareness.

MULTI-SENSOR FUSION ENGINE (MSFE)
       MSFE is the heart of sensor-synthesis.

       Fusion stages:

           1. harmonic fusion
                  HSC + HDO + HPC

           2. amplitude fusion
                  ADO + RED

           3. spectral fusion
                  SDO + SDW

           4. gradient fusion
                  GDO + GSF

           5. sync fusion
                  SFDO + SFC

           6. anomaly fusion
                  ADE + CEP

           7. storm fusion
                  SME + DRO

           8. turbulence fusion
                  TAE + clarity layers

           9. master synthesis
                  all fused into unified resonance-time field

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous synthesis
           • harmonic synthesis
           • braided synthesis
           • mesh synthesis

       MSFE produces the master resonance-time state.

CONSTELLATION COHERENCE INTERFACE (CCI)
       CCI synchronizes sensor-synthesis across platforms.

       CCI functions:
           • sync-field alignment
           • cross-platform fusion
           • constellation hazard sharing
           • distributed storm/turbulence mapping

       CCI ensures fleet-wide coherence.

RESONANCE-TIME MASTER RENDERER (RTMR)
       RTMR generates the final synthesized output.

       Rendering modes:
           • unified terrain mode
           • harmonic-structure mode
           • anomaly/storm mode
           • turbulence-flow mode
           • constellation mode
           • Picard-grade cartography mode

       RTMR provides the highest-fidelity RTT visualization.

PICARD-GRADE SYNTHESIS MODE
       A flagship synthesis mode combining:

           • full DRO stack
           • full CEP stack
           • HSC + ADE + SME + TAE
           • MSFE master fusion
           • CCI constellation alignment

       Capabilities:
           • complete resonance-time situational awareness
           • real-time harmonic epoch tracking
           • anomaly and storm pre-detection
           • turbulence-informed navigation
           • constellation-scale cartography

FAILURE MODES
       Synthesis may degrade due to:

           • overlay drift
           • clarity mismatch
           • harmonic collapse
           • spectral overload
           • gradient instability
           • sync-field fragmentation
           • storm/turbulence interference

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • synthesis-integrity score
           • fusion mismatch
           • clarity divergence
           • sync drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe synthesis states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • synthesis events
           • fusion states
           • anomaly/storm/turbulence signatures
           • harmonic transitions
           • constellation sync history

       Lineage enables long-term synthesis optimization.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • orchestrates synthesis
           • enforces safety thresholds
           • manages fusion layers
           • coordinates constellation coherence
           • blocks unsafe synthesis states

       WRSADC is the synthesis conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-synthesis is the master sensor architecture of RTT systems.
       It unifies overlays, clarity, harmonics, anomalies, storms, and turbulence
       into a single, coherent resonance-time intelligence layer.

